      <div style={{ backgroundColor: WHITE, padding: "3rem 0" }}>
        <div className="container" style={{ maxWidth: "900px" }}>
          <div className="content" style={{ color: "#363636" }}>
            <h2 id="the-proof"><i className="fa-solid fa-flag mr-2" style={{ color: "#00d1b2" }} aria-hidden="true"></i>The proof: it's a flag, not a chip</h2>
            <h3>The architecture</h3>
            <p>
              Signboard (the SL2T feature) is a <strong>Gboard feature split</strong> (
              <code>split_signboard.apk</code>, ~18.7 MB) inside{" "}
              <code>com.google.android.inputmethod.latin</code> — not a standalone app, not a
              system service.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>On-device:</strong> MediaPipe Holistic — face/hand/pose TFLite models in
                the split extract pose landmarks from the camera.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Translation:</strong> gRPC streaming to Google's cloud recognizer (
                <code>speechs3proto2-pa.googleapis.com:443</code>, the "S3" service) →{" "}
                <code>finalizedText</code> / <code>pendingText</code> back. An on-device
                recognizer fallback exists for offline (reduced quality).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>The server does not check your device or region.</strong> It accepts
                landmark coordinates and returns text.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h3>The gate</h3>
            <p>
              On a non-Pixel, a re-signed Gboard gets <strong>zero Phenotype flags</strong>{" "}
              (Google's server-delivered config). Without those flags, Gboard's{" "}
              <strong>ModuleManager availability system</strong> never marks the signboard
              module "available," so the <code>SignboardExtension</code> is never instantiated
              and the tile never appears — even though the code is sitting in the APK.
            </p>
            <p>
              I patched <strong>8 smali sites</strong> (all scoped by module ID, so no side
              effects on the other ~170 modules) and re-signed:
            </p>
            <div className="table-container">
              <table className="table is-bordered is-striped is-fullwidth">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>#</th>
                    <th>File</th>
                    <th>Patch</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>1</td>
                    <td>
                      <code>qtx.smali</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>PIXEL_2024 / PIXEL_2025 checks → true</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>2</td>
                    <td>
                      <code>urb.smali</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      <code>cr()</code> → true
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>3</td>
                    <td>
                      <code>rth.smali</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      <code>a()</code> → true (skip cert check)
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>4</td>
                    <td>
                      <code>rsh.smali</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      <code>l()</code> → true (is-Pixel check)
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>5</td>
                    <td>
                      <code>ezt.smali</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      <code>enable_signboard</code> default → 1
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>6</td>
                    <td>
                      <code>mmb.smali</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      inject "signboard" into <code>AccessPointsOrder</code>
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>7</td>
                    <td>
                      <code>pwl.smali</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      <code>j()Z</code> — force flag-ready for module ids <code>0x110</code>{" "}
                      (signboard) + <code>0x13c</code> (its dependency)
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>8</td>
                    <td>
                      <code>ModuleManager$ModuleInitListener.smali</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      force <code>areDependentModulesReady=true</code> for <code>0x110</code>
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <h3><i className="fa-solid fa-circle-check mr-2" style={{ color: "#00d1b2" }} aria-hidden="true"></i>Verification (authoritative)</h3>
            <p>
              <code>dumpsys input_method</code> on the S23 after the patch:
            </p>
            <pre>
              <code>{`ExtensionManager:
  {tag=ezt, class=class ezt, instance=ezt@1a6af99}   ← instantiated (matches Pixel)
AccessPointsOrder = [..., floating_keyboard, signboard, keyboard_resize, ...]
enable_signboard{type=Boolean, finalValue=true, DEFAULT=true}`}</code>
            </pre>

            <p>I signed to the front camera. Logcat:</p>
            <pre>
              <code>{`LandmarkProcessor.maybeMakeS3Request(): Making S3 request. frame count: 14
S3Handler: Sending S3Request. start: true, advance: true, frameCount: 14
S3Response, status: IN_PROGRESS, error code: 0
S3Response, status: DONE_SUCCESS, error code: 0
SignboardPopup.finishTranscription()`}</code>
            </pre>

            <div className="columns is-vcentered my-5">
              <div className="column is-half">
                <i className="fa-solid fa-thumbs-up fa-5x mb-3" style={{ color: "#00d1b2" }} aria-hidden="true"></i>
                <p>
                  <strong>
                    The S3 cloud recognizer is used (not the on-device fallback), and Google's
                    server accepted the S23.
                  </strong>{" "}
                  No region gate, no device gate. The entire feature was client-side gating; the
                  "server permission" is just the Phenotype rollout flag.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div className="column is-half">
                <video
                  controls
                  playsInline
                  style={{
                    width: "50%",
                    display: "block",
                    margin: "0 auto",
                    borderRadius: "6px",
                    border: "1px solid #dbdbdb",
                  }}
                  aria-label="Screen recording: signing to SL2T on the S23 Ultra, English text appearing on screen"
                >
                  <source src={sl2tVideo} type="video/mp4" />
                  Your browser does not support the video tag.
                </video>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3 id="appendix">Cross-platform verification</h3>
            <p>
              <strong>Emulator note:</strong> running this on an Apple Silicon Mac host needed
              an ARM64 system image (the signboard split ships an arm64-v8a native lib; it
              won't load under x86_64 emulation) and{" "}
              <code>-gpu host -feature GuestAngle</code> so MediaPipe's GPU delegate sees
              OpenGL ES 3.1 instead of the ES 3.0 that <code>-gpu host</code> alone exposes on
              macOS. That's an artifact of emulated GPU plumbing, not a hardware requirement of
              the feature itself — the S23 needed no GPU check to pass at all.
            </p>

            <p>
              <strong>Devices tested:</strong>
            </p>
            <div className="columns is-vcentered">
              <div className="column is-one-third">
                <figure className="image">
                  <video
                    controls
                    playsInline
                    style={{ width: "100%", borderRadius: "6px", border: "1px solid #dbdbdb" }}
                    aria-label="Screen recording: SL2T repro running on the ARM64 emulator, Apple Silicon Mac host"
                  >
                    <source src={sl2tMacEmulatorVideo} type="video/mp4" />
                    Your browser does not support the video tag.
                  </video>
                  <figcaption className="is-size-7 mt-2">
                    Same repro, independently confirmed on the ARM64 emulator running on an
                    Apple Silicon Mac host.
                  </figcaption>
                </figure>
              </div>
              <div className="column">
                <div className="table-container">
                  <table className="table is-bordered is-striped is-fullwidth">
                    <thead>
                      <tr>
                        <th>Target</th>
                        <th>Form factor</th>
                        <th>Result</th>
                      </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                      <tr>
                        <td>Galaxy S23 Ultra (SM-S918U), physical</td>
                        <td>Phone</td>
                        <td>
                          Signboard tile instantiated; S3 cloud recognizer completed end-to-end
                          (the screen recording above)
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <td>Galaxy S21 Ultra (SM-S911U), physical</td>
                        <td>Phone</td>
                        <td>Same 8 gate patches, same result</td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <td>Galaxy S26 Ultra (SM-S948U), physical</td>
                        <td>Phone</td>
                        <td>Same 8 gate patches, same result</td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <td>Galaxy S20 Ultra (SM-G996U), physical</td>
                        <td>Phone</td>
                        <td>Same 8 gate patches, same result</td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <td>ARM64 API-35 emulator, Apple Silicon Mac host</td>
                        <td>Phone (portrait)</td>
                        <td>
                          Same 8 gate patches, same result — independent confirmation on
                          unrelated silicon
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3>One honest split: feature vs. install</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>The feature is portable</strong> to any <code>arm64-v8a</code> / API 32+
                device. Proven on the S23 Ultra (Samsung/Qualcomm) and, independently, on an
                ARM64 emulator built and run on an Apple Silicon Mac (Google/ARM reference image
                on Apple silicon) — same patch set, same outcome, two unrelated vendors.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>The install is the device-dependent part.</strong> Where Gboard is a
                normal user app in <code>/data</code> (the S23 — I <code>adb install</code>'d it
                from the Pixel), swapping in the re-signed build is a one-liner, no root. Where
                the OEM ships Gboard as a locked <strong>
                  <code>/product</code> prebuilt
                </strong>{" "}
                (e.g. Pixel 8), replacing it hits a signature mismatch and removing the prebuilt
                needs root (<code>/product</code> is read-only on a locked bootloader).
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              So: the <em>feature</em> has no hardware limit. The <em>install</em> is a
              one-liner on most devices and needs root on <code>/product</code>-prebuilt ones.
              I'm not claiming "any phone, no root" — that would be false.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style={{ backgroundColor: GRAY, padding: "3rem 0" }}>
        <div className="container" style={{ maxWidth: "900px" }}>
          <div className="content" style={{ color: "#363636" }}>
            <h2 id="accessibility-issue"><i className="fa-solid fa-universal-access mr-2" style={{ color: "#00d1b2" }} aria-hidden="true"></i>Why this is an accessibility issue, not a mod</h2>
            <p>
              Sign language is the <strong>native language of a linguistic and cultural
              minority</strong>. UN CRPD Article 30(4) affirms the right to recognition and
              support of that linguistic identity, including sign languages. It is not a
              disability to be fixed; it is a language with its own grammar, history, and
              community.
            </p>
            <p>
              For decades, Deaf communication has run through a <strong>human relay</strong>:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>1880</strong> — the Milan Congress, where sign language was effectively
                banned from Deaf education by hearing outsiders.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>1964</strong> — the TTY (teletype) machine, built by a Deaf person,
                bridged Deaf and hearing over the phone line.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>1974 onward</strong> — relay services (TTY relay, then video relay),
                where a human interpreter is in the loop. (Video relay is free to the caller,
                funded through the FCC's TRS fund; a private in-person sign-language interpreter
                is a separate, much costlier option.)
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              SL2T is the <strong>first time the platform itself does the translation</strong> —
              no human in the middle. And the phone that can do it is the newest, most expensive
              one in Google's lineup.
            </p>
            <p>
              A Deaf person with a mid-range Android phone — which is what most of the world
              actually uses — can't have that moment. Not because their phone can't. Because a
              flag is off.
            </p>

            <h3><i className="fa-solid fa-comments mr-2" style={{ color: "#00d1b2" }} aria-hidden="true"></i>The community's own words</h3>
            <div className="scrolling-wrapper pb-4" style={{ whiteSpace: "nowrap" }}>
              <div
                className="card mb-4"
                style={{ display: "inline-block", width: "820px", whiteSpace: "normal", verticalAlign: "top", marginRight: "1.5rem" }}
              >
                <div className="columns is-vcentered is-mobile is-multiline p-4">
                  <div className="column is-two-fifths">
                    <figure className="image">
                      <img
                        src={calvinYoungPost}
                        alt="Calvin Young's LinkedIn post, &quot;My Deaf grandpa is 83 years old.&quot;"
                      />
                    </figure>
                  </div>
                  <div className="column">
                    <p style={{ color: "#363636" }}>
                      <strong>Calvin Young</strong> (on his 83-year-old Deaf grandfather trying
                      it for the first time): <em>"He signed. He signed. His words appeared on
                      the screen. He looked at me, smiled, and immediately wanted to try it
                      again."</em>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div
                className="card mb-4"
                style={{ display: "inline-block", width: "820px", whiteSpace: "normal", verticalAlign: "top", marginRight: "1.5rem" }}
              >
                <div className="columns is-vcentered is-mobile is-multiline p-4">
                  <div className="column is-two-fifths">
                    <figure className="image">
                      <img
                        src={christianeMendesPost}
                        alt="Christiane Nogueira Mendes's LinkedIn post about using SL2T at a Best Buy"
                      />
                    </figure>
                  </div>
                  <div className="column">
                    <p style={{ color: "#363636" }}>
                      <strong>Christiane Nogueira Mendes</strong> (on using it at a Best Buy):{" "}
                      <em>"Today, my hands became my voice on a screen."</em>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div
                className="card mb-4"
                style={{ display: "inline-block", width: "820px", whiteSpace: "normal", verticalAlign: "top" }}
              >
                <div className="columns is-vcentered is-mobile is-multiline p-4">
                  <div className="column is-two-fifths">
                    <figure className="image">
                      <img
                        src={ryanCommersonPost}
                        alt="Ryan Commerson's LinkedIn post, &quot;Open SL2T: The Strategic Case for API Release&quot;"
                      />
                    </figure>
                  </div>
                  <div className="column">
                    <p style={{ color: "#363636" }}>
                      <strong>Ryan Commerson</strong> (on the strategic case for an open API):{" "}
                      <em>"…the $200/hour interpreter with a 2-hour minimum loses its
                      gatekeeping power."</em> And a historical warning: if this stays closed,
                      the community repeats the pattern of waiting decades for access that only
                      arrives on the newest, most expensive hardware.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              <em>(Full posts linked in the Sources section.)</em>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style={{ backgroundColor: WHITE, padding: "3rem 0" }}>
        <div className="container" style={{ maxWidth: "900px" }}>
          <div className="content" style={{ color: "#363636" }}>
            <h2 id="why-posting">Why I'm the one posting this</h2>
            <p>
              I'm Deaf, and I've been modding Android since 2010. My earliest posts are of me
              getting <strong>Video Relay Services (VRS)</strong> working on phones that no longer exist —
              I was trying to make Deaf communication work on mobile a decade before this was a
              product. In 2011, I called Sprint for a data plan on my EVO 4G through a video
              relay interpreter — and the rep refused to even process the request because I'm
              Deaf. So I turned another phone into a free WiFi hotspot and got the EVO 4G online
              without them, just to keep relay working.
            </p>
            <p>
              I was making the Deaf-access argument on XDA in <strong>2011</strong> — posting
              about captions and speech recognition for Deaf users. A senior member,{" "}
              <strong>Azrienoch (Android Cues)</strong>, understood and kept sending me
              transcripts of his videos; <strong>Dsixda</strong>'s Android Kitchen later
              unblocked features on my phone that were already there but switched off, and I
              donated to it because it mattered.
            </p>
            <p>
              In <strong>2012</strong>, when <strong>Linus Torvalds</strong> asked whether
              anybody really cares about a tablet's front-facing camera, I answered that I do:
              it's what lets Deaf people use video relay on the go. Features that look like
              throwaways are often communication access. This is the same case. (
              <a href="#linus-source" onClick={scrollToSection("linus-source")}>
                See the original post
              </a>
              .)
            </p>
            <article className="message is-warning">
              <div className="message-body" style={{ fontSize: "1.25rem", fontStyle: "italic" }}>
                The sign-to-text code was already on the phone. It was just switched off for
                everyone but the Pixel 11.
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style={{ backgroundColor: GRAY, padding: "3rem 0" }}>
        <div className="container" style={{ maxWidth: "900px" }}>
          <div className="content" style={{ color: "#363636" }}>
            <h2 id="not-doing"><i className="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation mr-2" style={{ color: "#f14668" }} aria-hidden="true"></i>The risk</h2>
            <p>
              Re-signing Gboard goes against its terms of service, and Google can close this in
              the next update.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style={{ backgroundColor: WHITE, padding: "3rem 0" }}>
        <div className="container" style={{ maxWidth: "900px" }}>
          <div className="content" style={{ color: "#363636" }}>
            <h2 id="the-ask"><i className="fa-solid fa-bullhorn mr-2" style={{ color: "#00d1b2" }} aria-hidden="true"></i>The ask</h2>
            <p>
              The Deaf community asked for this <strong>in writing</strong>, in the AISLAC
              report: <strong>release the API, or release the weights.</strong> The answer so
              far is "we're exploring it."
            </p>
            <p>
              The infrastructure already exists on every phone.{" "}
              <strong>Ship it to them, or open it up so others can.</strong>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style={{ backgroundColor: GRAY, padding: "3rem 0" }}>
